/* Plain-language explanations shown by the "Explain it simply" button. p = simple paragraph, m = easy way to remember */
window.SIMPLE = {
/* ---------- DATABASE ---------- */
'db-cat':{p:'There are different kinds of databases for different jobs. A relational database keeps data in neat tables, like an Excel sheet with strict rules. NoSQL is more flexible, like a notebook where every page can look different. A graph database stores who is connected to whom, like a friends list. Also, some systems do many small quick jobs all day (OLTP, like an ATM), while others answer big questions about the past (OLAP, like "which month sold the most?").',m:'OLTP = the cashier, busy every second. OLAP = the manager, studying last year\'s reports.'},
'db-basics':{p:'Data is just raw facts, like "98.6". Information is when that fact gets meaning, like "the patient\'s temperature is normal". A database is the organised store of data. The DBMS is the software that looks after it (like MySQL). The database system is everything together: computer, software, data and apps. The miniworld is the small part of real life we are recording, like a university. Metadata is "data about data", for example a note saying the Name column holds text.',m:'Database = the cupboard, DBMS = the shopkeeper, database system = the whole shop.'},
'db-func':{p:'A DBMS does four main jobs, in order. First you define the design (which tables and columns). Then you construct, which means filling it with the first data. Then you manipulate, which means searching, adding, changing and deleting. Finally you share it safely, so many people can use it at once without mixing things up and only the right people can see each part. In the background it also keeps a log so it can recover after a crash, and picks the fastest way to answer each question.',m:'Define → Fill → Use → Share. (D-C-M-S)'},
'db-uni':{p:'This is the practice example used in class. A university keeps tables for students, courses, sections and grades. Each student has a unique ID (the primary key), so two students can never get mixed up. The grade table points to students using that same ID (a foreign key). A query is just a question to the database, like "show Smith\'s grades". A transaction groups steps together, like "enroll the student AND reduce one seat", so either both happen or neither does.',m:'Primary key = your roll number. Foreign key = writing someone else\'s roll number in your list.'},
'db-char':{p:'Four things make the database way better than plain files. 1) It describes itself: the design is stored inside, so the same software can run any database. 2) Programs and data are separate: you can change how data is stored without rewriting programs. 3) Different users see different views, like a student seeing only their own result while the office sees everyone\'s. 4) Many people can work at the same time without spoiling each other\'s work.',m:'Self-describing, Independent, Many views, Many users. (SIMM)'},
'db-actors':{p:'Many people are involved with a database. The DBA is like the guard and manager: gives permissions, makes backups and keeps it running. The designer plans the tables. Programmers build the apps people actually use. End users are of four types: naive users just press fixed buttons (a bank teller), casual users ask different questions now and then (a manager), sophisticated users are experts (a data scientist), and standalone users keep a small personal database. Behind the scenes, other people build the DBMS software, tools and hardware.',m:'Front stage: DBA, Designer, Programmer, Users. Back stage: builders, tool makers, operators.'},
'db-adv':{p:'A DBMS is good because each fact is saved only once, so there are no confusing copies. It stops wrong data from entering (like a GPA of 7.0). It can recover after a crash, sets common standards, and lets programmers build apps faster. But it is not always the right choice: it can be too expensive for a tiny project, too slow for machines that must react instantly (like an airplane autopilot), and too heavy for a simple one-person program where a plain file is enough.',m:'Use a DBMS for big, shared, important data. Skip it for tiny, single-user or split-second systems.'},
'db-hist':{p:'Databases changed a lot over time. In the 1960s data was stored like a family tree (hierarchical): one parent, many children. In the 1970s the network model allowed many parents using pointers. Then in 1970 Edgar Codd said: store data in simple tables and just ask WHAT you want, not HOW to get it. That idea gave us SQL and Oracle. Later came object databases, big-data tools from Google, NoSQL for huge websites, and today cloud databases. The future includes AI that tunes the database by itself and vector databases that power chatbots.',m:'Tree → Net → Tables (Codd 1970, SQL) → NoSQL → Cloud → AI.'},
'db-models':{p:'A data model is a way of describing data. There are three levels. The high level (conceptual) is how normal people think: "students take courses". This is drawn as an ER diagram. The middle level (representational) is how it becomes tables in a relational database. The low level (physical) is how it is actually saved on the disk as files and indexes. Each step goes closer to the machine.',m:'Idea (ER) → Tables (relational) → Disk (files).'},
'db-schema':{p:'The schema is the design or plan of the database: which tables and columns exist. It almost never changes. The state (or instance) is the actual data inside at this moment, and it changes all the time. Think of a classroom attendance register: the printed columns (Name, Roll no, Date) are the schema, and the ticks filled in today are the state.',m:'Schema = the empty form. State = the form filled in today.'},
'db-3schema':{p:'The three-schema architecture splits the database into three layers. The top (external) layer is what each user sees, like their own window. The middle (conceptual) layer is the full design for everyone. The bottom (internal) layer is how data is stored on disk. Between the layers there are mappings, like translators, so a change in one layer does not break the others.',m:'Users see the top floor, the plan is the middle floor, the storage is the basement.'},
'db-indep':{p:'Data independence means you can change one layer without breaking the layer above it. Physical independence: you change how data is stored (add an index, buy a faster disk) and your tables and programs keep working. Logical independence: you change the table design (add a column) and users\' views and programs still work. Logical is harder, because programs often depend on the exact table design.',m:'Physical = change the basement, the floors above don\'t notice. Logical = change the plan, the users don\'t notice.'},
'db-lang':{p:'A DBMS has languages to talk to it. DDL creates the structure (CREATE TABLE). DML works with the data (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE). SQL is high-level: you just say what you want and it finds it for you, many rows at once. Low-level languages fetch one record at a time inside a loop. There are also easy interfaces for people who don\'t know SQL: menus, forms, clickable screens, typing normal sentences, and simple buttons for staff like bank tellers.',m:'DDL builds the house, DML moves the furniture.'},
'db-util':{p:'Utilities are helper programs that come with a DBMS. Loading puts existing files into the database. Backup makes safety copies. Reorganization rearranges files to make them faster. Performance monitoring watches the system and tells the DBA what is slow. Extra tools include a data dictionary (a catalog of everything), app generators, CASE design tools, and network software so people can connect from far away.',m:'Load, Backup, Reorganise, Monitor. (LBRM)'},
'db-arch':{p:'This is about where the work happens. In a centralized system, one big computer does everything and users have simple screens. In two-tier client-server, your computer (client) talks directly to the database server. In three-tier, used by websites, there is a middle server between you and the database: the browser shows the page, the middle server runs the rules, and the database stores the data. That middle layer adds safety because you never touch the database directly.',m:'One box → Two boxes (client + DB) → Three boxes (browser + app server + DB).'},
'db-class':{p:'DBMSs can be grouped in many ways: by the data model they use (tables, documents, graphs), by how many users (one or many), by how many locations (one place or spread across many), by price (free or costly), by purpose (general or one special job like airline booking), and by type of work (many quick transactions, or big analysis reports).',m:'Ask six questions: What model? How many users? How many places? How much cost? What purpose? What workload?'},
'db-design':{p:'Designing a database is done in four steps. First you collect requirements by talking to users. Second you draw a conceptual design, usually an ER diagram. Third you turn that drawing into real tables (logical design). Fourth you decide the storage details like indexes (physical design). It is like building a house: listen to the family, sketch, make the blueprint, then choose bricks and pipes.',m:'Ask → Sketch → Tables → Storage.'},
'db-er':{p:'The ER model is a simple way to draw your data before making tables. Entities are things (a student, a car). Attributes are details about them (name, age). Some attributes can be broken into parts (address = street, city), some can have many values (several phone numbers), and some can be calculated from others (age from date of birth). NULL means the value is empty or unknown.',m:'Entity = noun, Attribute = its details, Relationship = verb that connects nouns.'},
'db-keys':{p:'An entity type is the general category, like "Student". The entity set is all the actual students stored right now. A key is a detail that is different for every one of them, like a roll number or CNIC, so you can always find exactly one person. If there are several such details, each one is a candidate key. The value set (domain) is the list of allowed values, like age must be 0 to 150.',m:'Key = something no two people share, like a CNIC.'},
'db-rel':{p:'A relationship connects entities, like "a teacher teaches a class". Degree is how many entity types are connected (two is most common). Cardinality tells the maximum: 1:1 (one principal for one school), 1:N (one department has many teachers), M:N (many students take many courses). Participation tells the minimum: total means everyone must take part (every employee must belong to a department), partial means it is optional (not every employee is a manager).',m:'Cardinality = "at most how many?". Participation = "must they join or not?"'},
'db-weak':{p:'A weak entity cannot be identified on its own. It needs an owner. Example: an employee\'s child (dependent). The child\'s name alone is not unique in the whole company, because two employees may both have a child named Ali. But "Employee 101\'s child Ali" is unique. So we use the owner\'s key plus the weak entity\'s partial key. In diagrams it is drawn with double lines.',m:'Weak entity = can\'t stand alone, needs its parent\'s name to be known.'},
'db-roles':{p:'When an entity joins a relationship, it plays a role. Usually this is obvious, but when the same entity type appears twice, roles are needed. Example: in "supervises", both sides are employees, so we say one is the Supervisor and the other is the Supervisee. Relationships can also have their own details. Hours worked on a project belongs to the pair (this employee + this project), not to either one alone.',m:'Same type twice? Give each side a role name.'},
'db-notation':{p:'ER diagrams use simple shapes. Rectangle = entity. Oval = attribute. Diamond = relationship. Double shapes mean "special": double rectangle is a weak entity, double oval is a multivalued attribute, double diamond is the weak entity\'s identifying relationship. An underlined name is the key. A dashed oval is a calculated attribute. A double line means everyone must participate; a single line means optional.',m:'Box = thing, Oval = detail, Diamond = action. Double = special.'},
'db-uml':{p:'UML class diagrams are another way to draw data, used more by software engineers. A UML class box has three parts: name, attributes and methods (actions). Links are plain lines instead of diamonds, with numbers like 1..* at the ends. UML has inheritance built in (an arrow with an empty triangle). ER diagrams are only about data and are made specifically for databases.',m:'ER = only data. UML = data plus actions.'},
'db-ternary':{p:'Sometimes three things must be linked together at once. Example: a supplier gives a part to a project. If you only save three separate pairs (supplier-part, part-project, supplier-project), you lose the exact fact of who gave which part to which project. So a ternary (three-way) relationship keeps that full fact. Only use it when all three really must be connected together.',m:'Three friends at one table tell the full story; three separate phone calls lose details.'},
'db-eer':{p:'Basic ER treats every entity as separate, so you repeat the same details again and again. EER fixes this with parent and child entities. The parent (superclass) holds common details, like Employee with name and salary. The child (subclass) adds its own details, like Instructor with highest degree. The child automatically gets everything the parent has. An instructor is still the same person as the employee record.',m:'Like a family: the child inherits the parent\'s surname and adds their own first name.'},
'db-specgen':{p:'There are two ways to build a parent-child hierarchy. Specialization goes top-down: start with a general group and split it, like Vehicle into Car, Truck and Motorcycle. Generalization goes bottom-up: start with separate groups, notice they are similar, and join them under one parent, like Savings Account and Checking Account into Account. Both end with the same design.',m:'Specialization = split down. Generalization = join up.'},
'db-constr':{p:'Two rules describe a hierarchy. Disjoint (d) means an item can be in only one child group: a vehicle is a car OR a truck. Overlapping (o) means it can be in several: an employee can be an engineer AND a manager. Total (double line) means every parent must belong to some child group: every student is undergraduate or postgraduate. Partial (single line) means some can belong to none: some employees are just general staff.',m:'d = only one, o = more than one. Total = everyone must, Partial = some can skip.'},
'db-union':{p:'A union type (category) is used when one thing can come from totally different parents. Example: the owner of a car can be a person, a bank or a company. These three have nothing in common, so they can\'t share one parent. So we make an "Owner" category that is a union of all three. Each owner only takes details from the parent it really is. It is drawn as a circle with a U.',m:'Union = "one of these different types", like an owner who could be a person, bank or company.'},
'db-relmodel':{p:'In the relational model, a relation is just a table. A tuple is one row (one record). An attribute is one column. The domain rule says each value must be the right type, like age must be a number. Entity integrity says the primary key can never be empty, because then you could not identify that row.',m:'Relation = table, Tuple = row, Attribute = column. Primary key is never empty.'},
'db-refint':{p:'A foreign key is a column that points to a row in another table, like DeptID in the Student table pointing to the Department table. Referential integrity means the pointer must be valid: the department must really exist, or the value must be empty. When someone deletes a department, you choose what happens: RESTRICT refuses the delete, CASCADE deletes its students too, and SET NULL keeps the students but clears their DeptID.',m:'A foreign key must point to something real, or nothing at all.'},
'db-mapping':{p:'This is the recipe for turning an ER diagram into tables. 1) Each normal entity becomes a table. 2) A weak entity becomes a table whose key is the owner\'s key plus its own partial key. 3) For one-to-one, put a foreign key on one side (best on the side where everyone participates). 4) For one-to-many, put the foreign key on the "many" side. 5) For many-to-many, make a new middle table holding both keys. 6) For a multi-valued attribute like several phone numbers, make a separate table.',m:'1:N → key goes to the many side. M:N → make a middle table.'},
'db-eermap':{p:'There are four ways to store parent-child data in tables. 8A: one table for the parent and one for each child, linked by the same ID (good general choice). 8B: only child tables, each repeating the parent details (only works if everyone is in exactly one child group). 8C: one big table with a "type" column, leaving empty spaces where details don\'t apply. 8D: one big table with yes/no flag columns, good when someone can be in several groups.',m:'More tables = fewer empty cells but more joins. One table = no joins but more empty cells.'},
'db-algebra1':{p:'Relational algebra is the maths behind database queries. Selection (σ) picks rows that match a condition, like "salary above 50,000". Projection (π) picks only some columns, like just name and salary, and removes duplicate rows. Rename (ρ) gives a table or column a new name, useful when you join a table with itself.',m:'σ picks Rows (Sigma = Select rows). π picks columns (Pi = Pick columns).'},
'db-algebra2':{p:'Set operations combine two tables like sets in maths. Union (∪) puts both together without repeats. Intersection (∩) keeps only rows that are in both. Difference (−) keeps rows in the first table but not in the second. These only work if both tables have the same columns. The Cartesian product (×) pairs every row of one table with every row of the other, so 3 rows × 4 rows gives 12 rows.',m:'∪ = all, ∩ = common, − = only mine, × = every pair.'},
'db-algebra3':{p:'A join connects two tables using a matching column. A theta join uses any condition (like >). An equijoin uses only equals. A natural join automatically matches columns with the same name and removes the duplicate column. Division (÷) answers "for ALL" questions, like "which students passed ALL the required courses?".',m:'Join = match and combine. Division = "who did ALL of them?"'},
'db-subq':{p:'A subquery is a question inside another question. A normal (uncorrelated) subquery runs once, like "find the average salary", and then the outer query uses that answer. A correlated subquery runs again for every row, because it depends on that row, like "is this employee paid more than the average of HIS OWN department?". EXISTS is fast because it stops as soon as it finds one match.',m:'Uncorrelated = ask once. Correlated = ask again for every person.'},
'db-joins':{p:'SQL joins decide what to do with rows that have no match. INNER JOIN shows only pairs that match in both tables. LEFT JOIN shows every row from the left table even if there is no match, filling the gaps with NULL. RIGHT JOIN does the same for the right table. FULL JOIN shows everything from both. To find departments with no students, use a LEFT JOIN and look for the empty ones.',m:'INNER = only couples. LEFT = all of the left side, matched or single.'},
'db-group':{p:'GROUP BY puts rows into groups, like all students of each department together. Then aggregate functions summarise each group: COUNT (how many), SUM (total), AVG (average), MIN and MAX. WHERE filters single rows before grouping. HAVING filters whole groups after grouping. So "departments with more than 50 students" needs HAVING, because the count only exists after grouping.',m:'WHERE = before grouping (rows). HAVING = after grouping (groups).'},
'db-trig':{p:'A trigger is an automatic rule inside the database. It follows ECA: an Event happens (insert, update or delete), a Condition is checked, and if true an Action runs. BEFORE triggers check data before it is saved. AFTER triggers record what changed, for example saving the old and new salary in a history table. Row triggers run for every row; statement triggers run once.',m:'Trigger = "WHEN this happens, IF this is true, THEN do that."'},
'db-assert':{p:'CHECK is a simple rule for one row, like "salary must be more than 0". An ASSERTION is a bigger rule across several tables, like "a department\'s total salaries can\'t go above its budget". Assertions are slow, so most real databases don\'t support them. Instead companies use triggers to enforce these big rules.',m:'CHECK = one row. ASSERTION = many tables (but in real life, use a trigger).'},
'db-views':{p:'A view is a saved query that looks like a table. It does not store data itself; it shows data from real tables when you open it. Views are used for security (show employees but hide their salary) and simplicity (hide a long complicated join behind one short name). You can only update through simple views. WITH CHECK OPTION stops you from adding a row that the view itself wouldn\'t show.',m:'A view is a window: you see the room, but the window holds nothing.'},
'db-ddl':{p:'These commands change the structure of a live table. ADD COLUMN adds a new column (give it a default value). DROP COLUMN removes one. MODIFY or ALTER COLUMN changes its type or whether it can be empty. DROP TABLE deletes a whole table: RESTRICT refuses if other tables point to it, CASCADE removes those links too. On very big tables this can lock the table, so it is done during quiet maintenance time.',m:'ALTER = renovate the table. DROP = demolish it.'},
'db-review':{p:'These are the four practice questions at the end of Chapter 3. Use 8A (separate tables) for a general hierarchy and 8C (one table with a type column) when children have few extra details. The foreign key goes on the "many" side because each of those rows points to exactly one parent. WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after. An AFTER UPDATE trigger automatically writes old and new values into an audit table, so nobody can hide a change.',m:'Practise writing these four answers from memory before the exam.'},

/* ---------- ENTREPRENEURSHIP ---------- */
'en-def':{p:'An entrepreneur is someone who starts and runs their own business by collecting money, people and materials to make something useful for society. The process is called entrepreneurship. Entrepreneurs spot chances to make new products, take careful (calculated) risks, work very hard, keep learning from failure, build good teams and create jobs. They are usually independent, positive, creative and comfortable when things are uncertain.',m:'Entrepreneur = sees a problem, takes a smart risk, builds a business that helps people.'},
'en-theory':{p:'Four theories explain why people become entrepreneurs. Trait theory: some people have the right personality (brave, creative, never give up). Opportunity theory: people notice a gap in the market and fill it, like opening a hostel near a university. Resource-based theory: people who can get money, materials and men (3M) start businesses. Social capital theory: people with strong contacts (family, friends, colleagues) get help, advice and funds more easily.',m:'Personality, Gap, 3M, Contacts. (Trait, Opportunity, Resource, Social)'},
'en-myths':{p:'Many things people believe about entrepreneurs are wrong. You don\'t have to be born one; you can learn. You don\'t have to be young, rich, a genius or alone. Entrepreneurs actually work more, not less. They take careful risks, not crazy ones. Many care about passion and helping others, not only money. You don\'t need a brand-new idea; improving an old one works. And many fail several times before they succeed.',m:'Almost every "entrepreneurs always…" sentence is a myth.'},
'en-barriers':{p:'Some things help or stop people from starting a business. Economic factors are about money and supplies: capital, workers, raw material, roads and a market that pays. Non-economic factors are social (family background and culture), psychological (no motivation, overthinking, can\'t dream big), political (government policies), and lack of business knowledge or skills.',m:'Money things (economic) + people things (social, mind, government, skills).'},
'en-intent':{p:'Intention means a plan in your mind that you are going to do something. Entrepreneurial intention is the reason that pushes you toward starting a business, like Careem\'s founder wanting to solve the taxi problem. People act on an idea only when it is desirable (they really want it) and feasible (they believe they can do it). That belief in yourself is called self-efficacy.',m:'Want it + believe you can do it = you will act.'},
'en-bg':{p:'Education gives you more options and makes success easier, but it doesn\'t decide whether you start a business; people like Mark Zuckerberg and Steve Jobs left their studies. Age matters a little: many people start at "milestone" ages like 25, 30, 35, 40 or 45. Men usually start in their early 30s and women in their mid 30s. Retired people often start businesses related to their old job.',m:'Education helps you succeed; it doesn\'t decide if you start.'},
'en-support':{p:'Entrepreneurs need people around them. A role model inspires you and can become your mentor, like Steve Jobs inspires many. Moral support comes from family and friends who cheer you up and keep you calm. Professional support comes from work contacts: mentors with experience, business associates who fill your skill gaps, suppliers, and trade associations of your industry.',m:'Role model = inspiration, family = cheering squad, professionals = practical advice.'},
'en-minority':{p:'More and more women and minority groups are starting businesses. Women are now starting businesses faster than men, for example frozen food, beauty parlours and stitching. This growth happens because these groups are being encouraged and because there are more role models for them to follow.',m:'More encouragement + more role models = more minority entrepreneurs.'},
'en-corp':{p:'Corporate entrepreneurship means acting like an entrepreneur inside a big existing company. A team inside the company creates a new business, using the company\'s money, name and resources. People who do this are called intrapreneurs. Google is a good example: it began as a search engine but its employees created Gmail, Maps and Meet. A company can innovate in two ways: change its strategy, or start a new venture.',m:'Entrepreneur = starts his own company. Intrapreneur = innovates inside someone else\'s company.'},
'en-renewal':{p:'Strategic renewal means a company changes its overall direction because the market has changed. It may change its business model (like a school moving from classrooms to online classes) or its structure (like letting teams make their own decisions). The goal is to stay competitive and survive for the long term.',m:'Strategic renewal = the company turns its steering wheel to follow the road.'},
'en-venturing':{p:'Corporate venturing means a company creates a brand-new business. Internal venturing (ICV) uses the company\'s own people, technology and money, and usually creates a new department. External venturing (ECV) means working with or investing in another company, and usually creates a joint venture. For example, one company gives technology and another gives cheap labour, and they start something together.',m:'ICV = do it ourselves inside. ECV = do it with partners outside.'},
'en-env':{p:'To make employees think like entrepreneurs, a company should value new ideas, hire creative people (like Google does), let managers and employees support each other, and not blame people for honest failures or threaten their jobs. Companies need this because new products help them survive when old ones decline, keep employees engaged, increase income, open new markets and meet customer expectations.',m:'No blame + creative people + support = new ideas.'},
'en-entry':{p:'A new entry means bringing something new to a market: a new product, an old product in a new place (like Careem coming to Bhakkar), or a brand-new company. Entrepreneurial strategy is the set of decisions you make for this entry: which customers, which resources, what price, how to beat competitors. It has three stages: create the opportunity, use the opportunity, and learn from the result to start again.',m:'New entry = new product, new place or new company.'},
'en-gen':{p:'To create a new entry, you need a special mix of resources that is Valuable (customers want it), Rare (few have it) and Inimitable (hard to copy), like Aamir Sweets\' secret formula. This mix comes from your own entrepreneurial skills, your market knowledge (what customers complain about), and your technological knowledge (what new machines and tools are coming).',m:'Your recipe must be Valuable, Rare and hard to copy (VRI).'},
'en-assess':{p:'Before entering, you check if the opportunity is worth it. The "window of opportunity" is the short time when conditions are perfect, like face masks during COVID. The problem is: if you spend too long collecting information, the window may close or a competitor may take it. If you enter and fail because you overestimated yourself, it\'s an error of commission. If you don\'t enter and later realise you could have succeeded, it\'s an error of omission.',m:'Commission = I did it and regret it. Omission = I didn\'t do it and regret it.'},
'en-firstmover':{p:'An entry strategy is your step-by-step plan for launching. Being the first mover (first in the market) has benefits: you can charge a higher price because there is no competition, your costs per unit fall because all customers come to you, you can lock in the best suppliers and shops, and you have time to satisfy customers. But people need time to learn about your product before you get a big market share.',m:'First in line gets the best price, the best suppliers and all the customers (for a while).'},
'en-fmdis':{p:'Being first also has risks. You don\'t know how many people will buy (market uncertainty). A better technology may come and beat you, like the first ATM company (Docutel) losing to newer ATMs (technological uncertainty). Customers may not trust a new product, so you must advertise, show benefits and give demos. Your "lead time" (the time with no competitors) is short, so you extend it with loyal customers, switching costs, a unique product and good supplier relations.',m:'First mover risks: will they buy? will tech change? will they trust me? how long am I alone?'},
'en-risk':{p:'Risk means the chance of losing money. One way to reduce risk is choosing your market scope. Narrow scope: sell a few special products to a small group, like a bakery selling only sugar-free bread. Less competition, but if that small market changes, you suffer. Broad scope: sell many products to many groups, like a makeup company selling lipstick, foundation and skincare. If one product fails, others save you, but you face more competitors.',m:'Narrow = small target, less competition. Broad = big target, less market risk.'},
'en-imitate':{p:'Imitation means copying what already works. It is safer because the idea is already proven. Franchising means buying the right to use a famous brand\'s formula, like opening a KFC; people trust the name immediately. Me-too strategy means copying a product with a small change, like a new ice-cream flavour. Imitation saves research costs and makes customers trust you from day one.',m:'Franchise = copy with permission. Me-too = copy with a small twist.'},
'en-trends':{p:'A trend is something many people are starting to buy, and a long trend is a great business chance. Wearable trend: things you can carry or wear, like smart watches. Green trend: eco-friendly things, like paper bags or electric cars. Maker trend: making things yourself and selling them, like custom cakes or stitching. Health trend: health services directly to people, like doctor video calls or gyms.',m:'Wear it, Green it, Make it, Heal it.'},
'en-sources':{p:'New business ideas come from four main places. Customers tell you what they need and what annoys them. Existing products can be improved, like inDrive adding price bargaining. Shopkeepers and distributors know what sells and what customers complain about. The government creates new needs through rules, like helmets for bikers or face masks during COVID.',m:'Listen to customers, improve products, ask shopkeepers, watch new laws.'},
'en-creative':{p:'Creativity is thinking of new useful ideas. It can fade with age, strict schooling and too many rules. Brainstorming: a group shares as many ideas as possible without criticising anyone, then picks the best. Reverse brainstorming: ask "how could we make this product terrible?" and then do the opposite to find hidden problems. Attribute listing: list the good and bad points of something and combine them into a better product, like auto-brightness phones to protect eyes.',m:'Brainstorm = many ideas, no judging. Reverse = imagine the worst, then fix it.'},
'en-innov':{p:'Creativity is having the idea; innovation is turning it into a real product. Four types: Incremental = small improvements every year, like each new phone model. Disruptive = a new way that takes over the market, like Amazon replacing bookshops. Architectural = using old technology for a new product, like putting phone technology into a watch. Radical = something completely new the world has never seen, like the airplane or the light bulb.',m:'Small step (incremental), take-over (disruptive), repackage (architectural), brand-new (radical).'},
'en-newprod':{p:'A product can be "new" in two ways. For the company, it is new if it improves performance and meets customer needs. For the customer, it is new if they feel it is different, even with a small change like a new colour or logo. Example: the Honda CD70 2017 and 2018 models only changed the indicator and logo, but customers saw a "new model".',m:'Firm asks: does it help our business? Customer asks: does it feel new to me?'},
'en-pdp':{p:'Before launching a product you go through steps and check it each time (market, competition, marketing, money and production). Idea stage: think of the product. Concept stage: describe it to people and ask their opinion. Product development: make a sample and let people use it. Test marketing: sell it in a small area to see real sales. Commercialization: launch it everywhere.',m:'Think → Ask → Sample → Test-sell → Launch.'},
'en-plc':{p:'Every product goes through a life cycle. Introduction: just launched, few buyers, high price. Growth: people hear about it, sales and profit rise fast. Maturity: sales are highest, many competitors appear, prices drop. Decline: people stop buying because needs are met or a better product arrived. To stay ahead, add new features during maturity, otherwise you end up like Nokia.',m:'Born, Grows, Peaks, Fades. (Intro, Growth, Maturity, Decline)'},
'en-ecom':{p:'E-commerce means selling online. It lets entrepreneurs reach more customers cheaply. You can do all the work yourself (in-sourcing), let another company do it all (outsourcing, like Shopify or Amazon), or mix both (hybrid). The front-end is what customers see (website, cart, checkout). The back-end is behind the scenes (stock, orders, delivery, payment). A beautiful website fails if the back-end is weak.',m:'Front-end = the shop window. Back-end = the warehouse and delivery.'},
'en-mobile':{p:'Most people now shop on phones, so your business must work well on mobile. A mobile website should have fewer features, less text and bigger buttons (for "fat fingers"). A dedicated app is even better: tasks take only 2–3 taps, young people love apps, and your icon on their screen reminds them of you every day. Mejuri and Chashmay are examples of businesses that sell mainly online.',m:'Mobile site = cut, cut, enlarge. App = fast, young, always visible.'},
'en-oap':{p:'To grow a business, an entrepreneur needs new opportunities, better management skills and a creative team. An opportunity assessment plan is a short plan that checks only the opportunity (not the whole business). It has four parts: the market (size, trends, growth), the idea and how it beats competitors, the team\'s skills, and a timeline for launching.',m:'Market, Idea, Team, Timeline.'},
'en-intl':{p:'International entrepreneurship means doing business in more than one country. The main benefit: if business is slow in one country, you can still earn in another. You can do it by exporting, licensing, or opening an office abroad. Because the world is a "global village", entrepreneurs must understand how other countries differ in politics, economy, society, technology, environment and law.',m:'More countries = more safety and more customers.'},
'en-econ':{p:'Every country\'s economy is different. Before entering, you should know how developed the country is, how strong its currency is, its business laws, whether its banks work well, if funding (venture capital) is available, its roads and internet, and its business culture and ethics. At home you already know all this; abroad you must learn it.',m:'Check development, currency, laws, banks, funding, infrastructure and ethics.'},
'en-bop':{p:'The Balance of Payments is a country\'s record of all money coming in and going out with the rest of the world. If more money comes in than goes out, it is a surplus. Entrepreneurs prefer countries with a surplus because their currency is stable, so business there is safer and more profitable.',m:'BOP surplus = more money coming in = stable currency = safer business.'},
'en-polegal':{p:'Politics and law abroad can hurt a business. Laws can suddenly change. The government may stop you sending profits home. Expropriation means the government takes your property for public use, like land for a road. Nationalization means the government takes over your whole company without fair payment. You must also know property rights (who owns a design), contracts (promises that law enforces), product safety rules, and product liability (paying if your product hurts someone).',m:'Expropriation = takes your land. Nationalization = takes your company.'},
'en-culture':{p:'Language is a big challenge abroad, so hire a translator who is a native speaker and understands business; bad translation can ruin your marketing. Technology can help you grow but you must adapt to new systems. Culture includes religion, politics, education, manners, language and social structure, and your product must respect all of these.',m:'Speak their language, respect their culture, adapt to their technology.'},
'en-motives':{p:'Businesses go global for many reasons: more profit, too much competition at home, a unique product, producing more than the home market can buy, falling sales at home, a special need abroad (like Pakistan needing solar panels from China), lower cost by producing more, learning new technology, and lower taxes in some countries.',m:'More profit, less competition, extra stock, special need, lower cost, new tech, lower tax.'},
'en-modes':{p:'There are several ways to enter a foreign market. Exporting: make it at home and sell abroad, either directly or through a local middleman. Licensing: let a foreign company make your product for a fee. Direct investment: own part of a business abroad (less than 50% is minority, more than 50% is majority), make a joint venture, or merge with a foreign company. Partnering: team up with a local entrepreneur who knows the market.',m:'Export (sell), License (rent your idea), Invest (own), Partner (team up).'},
'en-trade':{p:'Trade barriers make it harder or costlier to sell in another country. Tariffs are taxes on imports. Subsidies are government help to local businesses. Quotas limit how much can be imported. GATT (1947) is an agreement between over 100 countries to reduce these barriers. A Voluntary Export Restraint means an exporting country agrees to send less, like Pakistan limiting potato exports to Afghanistan. To avoid barriers, entrepreneurs may build factories in that country.',m:'Tariff = tax, Quota = limit, Subsidy = help for locals. GATT tries to remove them.'},

/* ---------- DATA MINING ---------- */
'dm-what':{p:'Data mining means digging through huge amounts of data to find useful hidden patterns, just like miners dig through soil to find gold. Today we create enormous data every second from phones, social media, shopping and sensors, and storage is cheap. But data is useless until we analyse it. Big companies like Google, Facebook and Amazon treat their data as their most valuable asset.',m:'Data mining = finding gold (patterns) in a mountain of data.'},
'dm-data':{p:'Data is a collection of objects (like customers) and their attributes (like age, city, income). Each row is an object and each column is an attribute. Attributes can be categorical (names or groups) or numeric (numbers). Categorical can be nominal (no order, like eye colour) or ordinal (has order, like small/medium/large). Numeric can be discrete (counts, like 3 children) or continuous (measurements, like 36.6°C).',m:'Row = object, Column = attribute. Nominal = no order, Ordinal = has order.'},
'dm-types':{p:'Data comes in different shapes. Record data is a normal table. Document data counts how many times each word appears (bag of words). Set data is like a shopping basket. Ordered data has a sequence: DNA letters or stock prices over time. Graph data shows connections, like web pages linking to each other.',m:'Table, Words, Basket, Sequence, Network.'},
'dm-vsdb':{p:'A normal database query asks an exact question and gets an exact answer, like "who bought milk?". Data mining asks a fuzzy question and finds patterns, like "what do people usually buy WITH milk?". Looking up a phone number is not data mining. Finding which customers are risky, or which customers behave alike, is data mining.',m:'Database = find exact facts. Data mining = discover hidden patterns.'},
'dm-tasks':{p:'Data mining tasks are of two kinds. Predictive tasks guess unknown or future values: classification (which group?), regression (what number?), time-series (what next?). Descriptive tasks describe the data: clustering (who is similar?), association rules (what goes together?), summarization and sequence discovery. Classification learns from labelled examples (supervised); clustering finds groups by itself (unsupervised).',m:'Predict the future vs Describe the present.'},
'dm-classif':{p:'Classification means putting new things into known groups. You first train a model on examples where the answer is already known, like past customers labelled "cheat" or "not cheat". Then the model guesses the group for new customers. You test it on separate data to check accuracy. It is used for targeted marketing, catching credit-card fraud and predicting which customers will leave.',m:'Learn from labelled examples, then label new ones.'},
'dm-cluster':{p:'Clustering means grouping similar things together without being told the groups first. Items in the same group are close to each other; items in different groups are far apart. Similarity is often measured with distance. Shops use it to group customers with similar habits, search engines group similar documents, and investors group stocks that move together.',m:'Birds of a feather flock together; clustering finds the flocks.'},
'dm-assoc':{p:'Association rules find things that happen together, written as "if X then Y". For example: if customers buy milk and eggs, they often buy cheese too. Supermarkets use this to place items near each other, plan promotions, and decide what happens if they stop selling a product. Repair companies use it to know which spare parts to carry together.',m:'"People who bought this also bought that."'},
'dm-regtask':{p:'Regression predicts a number instead of a group. For example: predicting sales from how much money was spent on advertising, predicting wind speed from temperature and pressure, or predicting tomorrow\'s stock price. Other tasks include finding unusual behaviour (deviation detection) and finding patterns that happen in a certain order over time.',m:'Classification answers "which group?"; regression answers "how much?"'},
'dm-kdd':{p:'KDD (Knowledge Discovery in Databases) is the whole journey from raw data to useful knowledge. Data mining is just one step in it. The steps are: Select the data you need, Clean it (fix missing or wrong values), Transform it into the right format, Mine it to find patterns, and Evaluate the results and show them clearly. Cleaning is often the hardest but most important step.',m:'Select → Clean → Transform → Mine → Explain. (S-C-T-M-E)'},
'dm-dev':{p:'Data mining grew out of several older fields: databases (SQL, data warehouses), statistics (regression, Bayes, k-means), information retrieval (search engines, similarity), machine learning (neural networks, decision trees) and algorithms. It faces problems like overfitting (memorising instead of learning), too much data, too many attributes, and messy, missing, noisy or changing data.',m:'Data mining = databases + statistics + search + machine learning.'},
'dm-slr':{p:'Simple linear regression draws the best straight line through data points so we can predict one thing from another. The formula is y = mx + c: m (slope) shows how much y goes up when x goes up by one, and c (intercept) is the value of y when x is zero. In the example, every extra hour of study adds 10 marks, starting from 30. So studying 5 hours gives 30 + 50 = 80 marks.',m:'y = mx + c → slope tells "how much more", intercept tells "where it starts".'},
'dm-lrlife':{p:'Linear regression is used everywhere in daily life. Social media creators use it to see which posting time gets the most likes. Property dealers use house size to estimate price. Students can predict marks from study hours. Shops predict next month\'s sales. Coaches see how much practice improves a player\'s accuracy.',m:'Whenever one thing grows with another, a straight line can predict it.'},
'dm-mlr':{p:'Multiple linear regression is the same idea but with several inputs instead of one. For example, salary depends on experience AND education. Each input gets its own weight (coefficient) that shows its effect while the others stay the same. We measure how wrong the line is using mean squared error, and find the best weights either with a direct formula (normal equation) or step by step (gradient descent).',m:'Simple regression = one cause. Multiple regression = many causes, each with its own weight.'},
'dm-mlrassume':{p:'Linear regression works well only if some conditions are true: the relationship is a straight line, the data points are independent, the errors are spread evenly, the inputs are not copies of each other, and the errors follow a normal bell shape. If two inputs are very similar (like house size and number of rooms), the model gets confused; this is multicollinearity. Regression is simple and fast but can\'t handle curves and is upset by outliers.',m:'Straight, Independent, Even errors, No twin inputs, Normal errors.'},
'dm-mlrex':{p:'This example finds a salary formula from experience and certifications using the normal equation. You put the inputs in a matrix X (with a first column of 1s), multiply and invert to get the weights. The result is: salary = 25 + 8.33 × experience + 6.67 × certifications. So someone with 5 years and 2 certificates is predicted to earn about 80. The method looks scary, but it is just careful multiplication step by step.',m:'Build X, compute XᵀX and Xᵀy, invert, multiply → weights.'},
'dm-learn':{p:'Learning means a system gets better with experience. Inductive learning means learning from examples, like showing a child many pictures of tigers until they can recognise a new tiger. Classification is a common learning problem: given features (big teeth, stripes), decide the category (tiger or not). Learning is like searching for the simplest rule that correctly explains all the examples.',m:'Show many examples → find a simple rule → use it on new cases.'},
'dm-dtree':{p:'A decision tree is like a game of 20 questions. You start at the top (root) with one question, follow the branch that matches your answer, ask the next question, and keep going until you reach a leaf that gives the final answer. For example: Is it sunny? If yes, is humidity high? If yes, don\'t play tennis. Nodes are questions, branches are answers, and leaves are results. Pruning means cutting extra branches to keep the tree simple.',m:'Root = first question, Branch = answer, Leaf = final decision.'},
'dm-chaid':{p:'CHAID is an older way to build decision trees using the chi-square test. It compares what actually happened (observed) with what we would expect by chance (expected). If the difference is big, the split is important. The formula is: chi-square = sum of (observed − expected)² ÷ expected. A higher value means a better, more meaningful split.',m:'Big difference between observed and expected = useful split.'},
'dm-id3':{p:'ID3 builds a decision tree by always choosing the "best question" first. To find the best question it uses entropy, which measures how mixed up the data is: 0 means all the same answer (pure), and higher means very mixed. Information gain shows how much a question reduces this mess. ID3 picks the attribute with the highest information gain, splits the data, and repeats for each branch.',m:'Entropy = messiness. Information gain = how much a question cleans the mess.'},
'dm-id3ex':{p:'In the tennis example there are 14 days, 9 "play" and 5 "don\'t play", so the starting entropy is 0.940. We calculate the gain of each weather feature: Outlook gives the biggest gain (0.246), so it becomes the first question. Overcast days are always "play". For sunny days, humidity decides. For rainy days, wind decides. The final tree has only three questions.',m:'Outlook first. Sunny → humidity. Rain → wind. Overcast → always play.'},
'dm-id3char':{p:'ID3 is fast and simple, but it has weaknesses. Because it always grabs the best question right now (greedy), it may miss the best overall tree. It can overfit, meaning it memorises the training data too closely, so smaller trees are better. It usually makes small trees but not always the smallest. It also struggles with continuous numbers because there are too many places to split.',m:'Greedy, may overfit, not always smallest, bad with continuous numbers.'},
'dm-nnintro':{p:'Neural networks are computer systems inspired by the human brain. The brain has billions of connections working in parallel, is slow but very smart, and keeps working even if some cells die. Computers are very fast but work step by step and aren\'t truly intelligent. Neural networks try to combine both: they learn from examples instead of being programmed for every rule. They are used both to understand the brain and to build smart machines.',m:'Neural network = a mini brain made of maths that learns from examples.'},
'dm-neuron':{p:'A real brain cell (neuron) has dendrites that receive signals, a cell body that processes them, an axon that sends the signal out, and synapses where it connects to other neurons. The brain has around 10 to 100 billion neurons, each connected to thousands of others. In an artificial network, the strength of each synapse is copied as a "weight".',m:'Dendrite = ear, Cell body = brain, Axon = mouth, Synapse = handshake (weight).'},
'dm-annbasics':{p:'An artificial neuron multiplies each input by a weight, adds them up with a bias, and passes the total through an activation function (like the sigmoid curve) to give an output. A network is described by its shape (architecture), how it learns (training) and its activation function. In feed-forward networks, signals go one way from input to output; in recurrent networks they can loop back. Supervised training uses correct answers; unsupervised training finds groups by itself.',m:'Input × weight + bias → activation → output.'},
'dm-annapps':{p:'Neural networks can do many jobs: complete a broken or noisy picture, classify faces as male or female, find good-enough answers to hard puzzles like the travelling salesman problem, compress data, learn input-output rules, link similar inputs to similar outputs, predict the next stock price, and control robots.',m:'Complete, Classify, Optimise, Compress, Approximate, Associate, Predict, Control.'},
'dm-perceptron':{p:'A perceptron is the simplest neural network: inputs with weights go into one output that says +1 (yes) or −1 (no). It learns by trying: if the answer is wrong, it adjusts the weights a little toward the right answer; if correct, it changes nothing. In the AND example it starts with all weights 0 and after a few rounds ends with w1 = 2, w2 = 3, bias = −4, which correctly gives "yes" only when both inputs are 1. Its weakness: it can only separate data with a straight line.',m:'Wrong answer → nudge the weights. Right answer → leave them.'},
'dm-mlp':{p:'A multilayer perceptron (MLP) adds one or more hidden layers between input and output. This lets it solve problems a single perceptron can\'t, because it can draw curved boundaries. The hidden layer is called "hidden" because we never see or set its correct values directly. The history: the first neuron model in 1943, the perceptron in 1962, its limits shown in 1969, and backpropagation in 1986 made MLPs trainable.',m:'Input → hidden layer(s) → output. Hidden layers let the network learn curves.'},
'dm-bp':{p:'Backpropagation is how an MLP learns. First the forward pass: the input travels through the network to produce an answer. Then we compare it with the correct answer to find the error. Then the backward pass: the error is sent back through the network, and each weight is adjusted a little to reduce the error. Repeat this many times with many examples. It works well but can be slow and sometimes gets stuck in a "local minimum".',m:'Guess forward, measure the mistake, fix backward, repeat.'},
'dm-bpex':{p:'This example shows backpropagation with real numbers. Inputs 0.05 and 0.10 should give outputs 0.01 and 0.99. With the starting weights, the network outputs about 0.75 and 0.77, a total error of 0.298. We work backwards using the chain rule to see how much each weight caused the error, then move each weight a little (learning rate 0.5). After one round the error drops to 0.291, and after 10,000 rounds it is almost zero (0.000035).',m:'Forward → error → chain rule backward → update weights → error shrinks.'}
};
